.TH RPCSERVE 1
.SH NAME
rpcserve \- a conventional RPC server, for comparison against Styx
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rpcserve
[
.B -v
]
[
.I address
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rpcserve
answers a small request-response protocol on
.I address
.RB ( tcp!*!6667
by default).  It exists to be measured against, by
.IR styxbench (1)'s
.B -r
arm.
.PP
.B doc/inferno-cloud-modern.ms
item 8 asks what a Styx round trip costs "against a conventional RPC
with its own framing and serialisation, which is the thing a real
service would otherwise use".  Raw TCP \(em a write and a read with no
protocol at all \(em is the floor, and is not what anyone would deploy.
This is the other point of comparison.
.PP
The protocol is deliberately small and ordinary:
.IP
.EX
request:  4-byte big-endian length, then "GET <offset> <count>\en"
reply:    4-byte big-endian length, then "OK <n>\en" then n bytes
.EE
.PP
A length prefix and a text header is what most hand-rolled RPCs and
every JSON-over-TCP service amount to.  What is being measured is
having to produce and parse them on both sides.
.I Offset
is accepted and ignored: the payload is generated rather than read from
anywhere, because reading a disk on one side only would measure the
disk.  It is in the protocol so that a request has more than one field
to parse, which is the point.
.PP
One connection is served at a time, to completion, because the
measurement it exists for uses one.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/rpcserve.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxbench (1),
.IR tcpserve (1),
.IR styxlisten (1)
.SH BUGS
It is not a general-purpose RPC server and should not be used as one:
there is one method, no authentication, no concurrency and no attempt
at a schema.
.PP
It is not HTTP, on purpose.
.IR httpclient (2)
here sends
.BR "Connection: close" ,
so measuring against it would cost a new TCP connection per request
while the Styx arm reads an already-open fid \(em which would flatter
Styx by a whole connection setup rather than comparing protocols.
